ion and subsequent Python 2.4 syntax correction were directly connected to the required service; the init script, service enablement, and reboot supported operation and mandatory restart evidence. Temporary verification files were cleaned, and the fixed snapshots show no new /tmp residue or platform change beyond the expected boot identity. The initial incompatible syntax was corrected in place and was not an unrelated experiment.","evidence_ids":["cmd-85d35d8bb34e47d7","cmd-8c161660df2247ef","cmd-e3e82aa70ed74c99","cmd-3a1005b0dff74513","cmd-b2994ca0160d465b","global:after_prepare:node1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are satisfied with direct behavioral evidence. The provider-wide restart protocol is complete for node1: it includes a healthy pre-restart state and boot ID, restart acknowledgement, observed outage, a distinct post-restart boot ID independently confirmed by lifecycle data, and recovered service, content, report data, isolation, release, and kernel state.","limitations":["The fixed lifecycle listening-socket observation is empty after execution even though multiple direct executor checks show 0.0.0.0:8080. Because the same lifecycle collector also omitted known baseline sockets, this appears to be a collector limitation, but it is a minor evidence inconsistency.","The lifecycle package-health probe invokes an unsupported yum operation and therefore cannot independently establish package consistency; its output is unchanged before and after execution, and no package mutation appears in the executor timeline."]}
